  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years",
  "hero.sub": "A seven-pin data cable and a set of rules fixed in 2004,<br>why are they still what the largest hard drives plug into in 2026?",
  "hero.lead": "The second question follows from the first. When a computer says “read sector 123456”, what does that sentence turn into, how does it travel down the wire, and how does the data come back? We start with a parallel ribbon cable and take it apart layer by layer: why the bus became serial, why the 0s and 1s on the wire are re-encoded, how one read command is cut into frames, why the drive queues commands itself, how it saves power when idle, and why it stopped at 6 Gb/s. <strong>Each model is either a real rule from the specification or a published result</strong>, scaled down so it runs in a web page.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The numbers in the specification (frame formats, the encoding table, the time limits) are taken directly from the text of SATA 3.5a and ATA/ATAPI-7. <strong>Where a value is marked as a “tuned parameter”, it was chosen so the model can be computed, not measured.</strong> The text says how far the conclusion moves when that value doubles. This page covers mechanisms and specifications only, and <strong>does not rate any drive as a purchase</strong>. Why NVMe is faster is covered in <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”</a>, and this page points back to it where needed.",

  "toc.ch1": "Where SATA sits on the map",
